For the test, lines lie along anti-diagonals (constant r+c): s = 3(1), 9(8), 19(9), 25(6), 27(4), 29(4), 35(2) — 7 lines, and mod 7 these give keys 3,2,5,4,6,1,0, a full permutation. So output(r,c) = P[(r+c) mod 7] with P = [2,4,8,1,6,9,4]. I'll detect the orientation automatically (the one giving fewer distinct diagonals).

```python
def transform(grid):
    R = len(grid); C = len(grid[0])
    cells = [(r, c, grid[r][c]) for r in range(R) for c in range(C) if grid[r][c] != 0]
    def diagonals(f):
        d = {}
        for r, c, v in cells:
            d.setdefault(f(r, c), set()).add(v)
        return d
    da = diagonals(lambda r, c: r - c)
    db = diagonals(lambda r, c: r + c)
    if len(da) <= len(db):
        f, ds = (lambda r, c: r - c), da
    else:
        f, ds = (lambda r, c: r + c), db
    n = len(ds)
    P = {}
    for d, vs in ds.items():
        k = d % n
        for v in vs:
            P[k] = v
    return [[P[(f(r, c)) % n] for c in range(C)] for r in range(R)]
